**Open Question (Two-input representation and original-constructor boundary)** · `open:nrz:matrix-loans-binary-implementation-boundary`

The binary recipe and original correspondence constructor are prerequisites of the matrix operation surface selected here; the scalar primitives alone do not supply them. The chosen slice must implement and independently inventory that original checked constructor as well as the shared two-pin adapter. Its symbolic Work depends on the supplied original region/descriptor construction and must not be represented by the masked admission's fixed figures. The side size 1592 and unchanged shared enum layouts require the independent ABI mirror specified above; the side box is part of this one common mechanism, not a retained native program. If that representation cannot preserve the unary widths, or original correspondence needs a retained authority/program beyond the explicitly inventoried box, stop at this boundary and report the dependency. The smaller next slice is the shared two-input mechanism with Product and the same complete ownership/resource acceptance, followed by Quotient's domain/dispatch controls. A second lifetime mechanism, certificate program, changed unary pins or a broadened input representation is not an acceptable way to force the pair into one implementation commit.

## Published results as unary inputs · `sec:nrz:matrix-loans-chains`

**Decision (Chained masked applications through one unary input)** · `dec:nrz:matrix-loans-chained-applications`

Admit a published binary64 center/total/error result as the single input of Square, SquareRoot or Reciprocal. Square also admits binary32 continuation under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-mapped-square`), except that binary32 Product and Quotient results refuse chained consumers with `UnsupportedContract`; a chained Square refusal preserves the original pin and makes zero numerical reads or allocation attempts (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-binary32-binary-chain-refusal]`). Its producer may be one of those unary members, or an ordered host-origin Product or Quotient under (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-binary-members`). A unary producer may itself consume an admitted result. Mapped-copy producers also preserve current lineage under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-mapped-movement`). This admits Product followed by SquareRoot followed by Reciprocal, the represented-input numerical chain needed for angular frequency under (`req:nrz:lc-evaluation-kernel-interface`), without claiming the physical LC binding, guard or graph contract in (`req:nrz:lc-evaluation-matrix-owner`). Choose this slice because a unary consumer already has one exact source capture and one transferred pin; the existing binary producer already seals both original operands. A binary consumer with a chained operand would also extend its original correspondence constructor and ordered source validation. Leave that extension separate under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-chain-outstanding`).

Add opt-in `Heap::request_chained_pointwise_square`, `request_chained_pointwise_square_root` and `request_chained_pointwise_reciprocal`, with Square taking `PointwiseRequest<P>` and returning `ApplicationRequest<P>` in either native format; SquareRoot and Reciprocal take `PointwiseRequest<f64>` and return `ApplicationRequest<f64>`. They select the expected unary member and require application origin. Existing host entry points, including binary and prepared entry points, retain their accepted inputs, application-origin refusals, charged prefixes and controls unchanged. An internal admission mode selects the origin check; it introduces no persistent field, public completion constructor or separate planner. Use the same detached core, selected reader, traversal, publisher, cancellation and reconciliation. The actual pinned input's immutable origin selects composition validation on publication; a request cannot turn it off. The three opt-in entry points implement this unary-consumer slice through the common application core.

**Rule (Numerical error publication scope)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-error-scope`

Application evidence records `NumericalErrorScope`, and `TileReader::numerical_error_scope` exposes it independently of source retirement. Host companions have `HostInput` scope. Mapped movement inherits the immediate input scope and copies its error bit-for-bit: a host copy reports `HostInput`, and a copy of an arithmetic result reports `LocalOperation`. The inherited scope is authenticated while the source is retained and survives its retirement (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-copy-square-error-scope]`). Arithmetic has `LocalOperation` scope: its Error bounds only the current operation's numerical discrepancy; inherited uncertainty participates in Total through the input total radius. Error is not a whole-chain numerical bound. The capture seal authenticates a copy's inherited scope against its retained source before evaluation. The member seal authenticates arithmetic scope before evaluation and publication. Literal small-integer Square continuations pin the local error to the independently encoded next float above the squared centre's upper ulp, after copying nonzero incoming error (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-mapped-square-gathered-both-formats]`), (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-mapped-square-product-and-outside-reader]`); copied errors retain their literal values (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-mapped-movement-original-companions]`). Whole-expression numerical-error propagation remains open.

**Rule (Current origin and original correspondence for composition)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-chain-origin`

First authenticate the supplied pin's continuation account and destination account, exact heap scope, live published group, pin-index pairing and numerical format through the current reader machinery in `loan/reader.rs`. Require `RepresentedInputs` in the member's admitted binary format, three declared ordinary roles, identity local incidence, equal C by k extents with k positive and the same original `CellAxis`. Admit only the five producer kinds named in (`dec:nrz:matrix-loans-chained-applications`); prepared evidence, retained law uses and stored endpoint attachments are unsupported. Match the group's application debit alternative and `Published` producer receipt to the bundle identity and origin. Validate the origin with `Origin::validate_current`: original checked operation and captured law pointer, member and revision, exclusion witnesses, source declaration and attachments, destination identity, completed-mask pointer and outcome extent must agree. For a binary producer this includes its side token, ordered original correspondence, both captured operands and empty published right-pin slot. Host import validation alone cannot establish this evidence, and equal readings, shapes or descriptor dimensions cannot replace it.

Composition additionally requires the recorded numerical lineage to be current. Starting at the authenticated immediate result, follow each captured `source_identity` to the exact published registry group; compare its declaration, value/companion symbols, qualified attachments, axis, extent, coverage and format with the capture, then run the same current-owner validation used by `reader.rs`. A unary ancestor continues this walk. A binary ancestor terminates it after validating Left and Right as the two distinct host origins required by (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-binary-correspondence`). A host terminates its branch. Check each group's origin against its host/unary/binary inventory and terminal receipt, including the absence of application evidence for a host. Reuse the immutable member evidence; do not rerun a kernel, rebuild an original operation, derive descriptors again or resolve a retired ordinary demand mask. This extra eligibility check belongs only to new composition and its publication. Ordinary current readers, ticket-to-pin acquisition and historical readers retain their source-independent behavior in (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-pointwise-states`), (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-binary-lifetime`). Thus a retained result can remain readable after its sources retire while being ineligible for a new chain.

